  <p><strong>Our pick for most kids:</strong> the DQ Sandwich. It is the cheapest treat on the menu and one of the lightest, and there is no melting cone to chase. For a Blizzard, stick with the Mini. A Small Oreo Blizzard is about 420 calories, which is a lot for a young child after a meal. Summer flavors like Cotton Candy cost more, at about $4.49 for a Mini.</p>
  <p>Adding a Mini Blizzard to a kids meal brings the total to about $8.48. More cones, Dilly Bars and sundaes are on the <a href="/categories/classic-treats">classic treats menu</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="allergens">Allergen pointers for parents</h2>
  <p>Every kids meal contains wheat and soy, and the cone adds milk to all three. Here is what each meal lists:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kids Cheeseburger Meal:</strong> milk, wheat, soy. The cheese adds milk to the entrée, not just the cone.</li>
    <li><strong>Kids Chicken Strips Meal:</strong> milk, wheat, soy and eggs.</li>
    <li><strong>Kids Hot Dog Meal:</strong> wheat and soy, with milk only from the cone. It has the shortest allergen list, so for a dairy-sensitive child, order it with no cone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A few more things to know:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The vanilla cone lists milk, wheat and soy. The Dilly Bar lists milk and soy.</li>
    <li>The Non-Dairy Dilly Bar is made without milk, but it may be made in a facility that also handles milk.</li>
    <li>The M&amp;M’s Blizzard may contain peanuts. Check before ordering for a child with a peanut allergy.</li>
    <li>Blizzards are blended on shared mixers, and fries often share oil with breaded chicken, so nothing made in the restaurant is guaranteed allergen-free.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Always check DQ’s official allergen information and talk to the store for a serious allergy. Our <a href="/blog/dq-gluten-free-allergen-guide">DQ gluten-free and allergen guide</a> goes into more detail.</p>

  <h2 id="kids-meal-vs-deals">Kids meal vs. $7 Meal Deal vs. 2 for $5 for older kids</h2>
  <p>For a young child, the kids meal is the best buy. For a hungry older kid or teen, the <a href="/items/7-meal-deal">$7 Meal Deal</a> is usually worth the extra $2.01, and the <a href="/items/2-for-5-mix-match">2 for $5 Mix &amp; Match</a> works for a big snack.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="size-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What you get</th><th>Price</th><th>Calories</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Kids meal</strong></td><td>Kid-size entrée, fries or applesauce, milk or soft drink, small cone</td><td class="price-cell">$4.99</td><td>~650 to ~750 (with fries)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>$7 Meal Deal</strong></td><td>Original Cheeseburger or 3-piece strips, regular fries, soft drink, small sundae</td><td class="price-cell">$7.00</td><td>~900 (burger) or ~1,100 (strips)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>2 for $5</strong></td><td>Any two eligible items, e.g. 2-piece strips plus a cheeseburger</td><td class="price-cell">$5.00</td><td>~680 for that pair</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick the kids meal</strong> for younger kids. It is the only option with both a drink and a dessert under $5.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick the $7 Meal Deal</strong> for a teen who eats like an adult. The chicken version has 3 strips instead of 2, adult-size fries and a sundae instead of a small cone.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick 2 for $5</strong> when your older kid wants a big snack rather than a full meal. There is no drink or dessert included. The eligible list varies by store, so check what yours offers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Our <a href="/blog/dq-meal-deals-guide">DQ meal deals guide</a> breaks down each deal, and the <a href="/combos">DQ deals page</a> lists every current combo in one place.</p>
